Chapter 2: Epistemic Foundations

2.1 The Four Constraints

The framework begins with four constraints:

\boxed{\text{No source}\Rightarrow\text{No numerical claim}}

\boxed{\text{No definition}\Rightarrow\text{No variable}}

\boxed{\text{No provenance}\Rightarrow\text{No verification}}

\boxed{\text{No admissible aggregation}\Rightarrow\text{No aggregate total}}

These constraints are not empirical hypotheses. They are methodological admissibility rules. A reported number is therefore not automatically a measurement suitable for aggregation. Every quantity must have an epistemic type.

2.2 Methodological Rules versus Empirical Hypotheses

\boxed{ \text{Methodological rule} \neq \text{Empirical hypothesis} }

Methodological rules are protocol conditions that define what counts as admissible measurement. For example, "No source ⇒ no verified number" is a protocol rule. It cannot be empirically falsified because it is a definitional condition of the measurement system.

Empirical hypotheses are substantive claims about the world that can be tested against evidence. For example:

H_1: \text{a particular chokepoint variable is sufficient to predict downstream loss}

H_2: \text{a propagation coefficient is stable across a defined class of conflicts}

These can be empirically tested and potentially falsified. The distinction protects the framework against confusion between method and result.

2.3 The Rejection of "One True Number"

\boxed{ \text{One conflict} \rightarrow \text{many estimands} }

rather than:

\text{One conflict} \rightarrow \text{one true cost number}.

A conflict may have legitimate estimands including incremental fiscal expenditure, lost real output relative to a specified counterfactual, replacement cost of destroyed infrastructure, welfare loss measured in equivalent income, and others. Each estimand requires its own measurement architecture, its own admissibility conditions, and its own uncertainty quantification.

2.4 Formal Coherence and Empirical Validity

The architecture itself should be treated as a research object. Its validity is not established by mathematical elegance. It must survive empirical attempts to break it.

\boxed{ \text{Formal coherence} \neq \text{empirical validity} }

\boxed{ \text{Cross-conflict portability} = \text{empirical question} }

