Chapter 3: The Typed Measurement Ontology

3.1 The Measurement Types

Define:

\mathcal X= \mathcal X_{\rm obs} \dot\cup \mathcal X_{\rm est} \dot\cup \mathcal X_{\rm der} \dot\cup \mathcal X_{\rm cf}

where:

Observation ($x\in\mathcal X_{\rm obs}$) is a directly recorded quantity associated with a defined measurement procedure. Examples include recorded expenditure, measured production, documented physical destruction, recorded displacement, and observed mortality.

Estimate ($x\in\mathcal X_{\rm est}$) is produced through statistical, econometric, engineering or expert estimation.

Derived quantity ($x\in\mathcal X_{\rm der}$) is obtained through an explicit transformation $x_j=f(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$. All inputs must themselves be traceable.

Counterfactual quantity ($x\in\mathcal X_{\rm cf}$) represents an alternative state of the system under a specified counterfactual design.

3.2 Typing as Provenance Tags

These categories should be treated as provenance tags rather than a rigid disjoint union. A single data point can carry multiple tags. For example, a mortality figure may simultaneously be an estimate (statistical inference), derived (from survey data), and counterfactual-dependent (involving assumptions about what would have occurred absent the conflict).

3.3 Formal Definition of the Typed Measurement System

Definition 3.1 (Typed Measurement System). A typed measurement system is a tuple

\mathcal T = (\mathcal X, \tau, \preceq)

where:

· $\mathcal X$ is a set of quantities;
· $\tau: \mathcal X \rightarrow 2^{\{\text{obs}, \text{est}, \text{der}, \text{cf}\}}$ assigns a non-empty set of provenance tags to each quantity;
· $\preceq$ is a partial order on $\mathcal X$ representing dependency: $x \preceq y$ means $x$ is an input to $y$.

Axiom 3.1 (Dependency Closure). If $x \preceq y$ and $\text{der} \in \tau(y)$, then $\tau(x) \neq \varnothing$.

Axiom 3.2 (Counterfactual Grounding). If $\text{cf} \in \tau(x)$, then there exists a counterfactual design $D_{CF}$ such that $x$ is defined relative to $D_{CF}$.

3.4 Provenance Tags and Their Formal Properties

Proposition 3.1. The provenance tags form a join-semilattice under set union:

\tau(x) \vee \tau(y) = \tau(x) \cup \tau(y)

Proof. The union operation is associative, commutative, and idempotent. The empty set serves as the identity element for quantities with no provenance tags, though Axiom 3.1 prohibits derived quantities from having empty tag sets. ∎

3.5 The Minimal Core Measurement Object

The full architecture requires six graphs, provenance chains, compatibility vectors, and counterfactual designs. This is theoretically complete but operationally heavy. To address adoption risk, we define a minimal core Measurement Object that can stand alone.

Definition 3.2 (Minimal Core Measurement Object). A minimal core Measurement Object is a tuple

M_i^{\rm core} = (V_i, T_i, P_i, S_i, \operatorname{Status}_i)

where:

· $V_i$ is the measured value (point or interval);
· $T_i \in 2^{\{\text{obs}, \text{est}, \text{der}, \text{cf}\}}$ is the set of provenance tags;
· $P_i$ is the provenance record;
· $S_i$ is the scope;
· $\operatorname{Status}_i$ is the verification status.

Proposition 3.2 (Core Sufficiency). The minimal core Measurement Object is sufficient for a single, non-aggregated numerical claim.

Proof. A single numerical claim requires: (i) a value, (ii) a type indicating how it was produced, (iii) provenance for verification, (iv) scope for interpretation, and (v) a verification status. The minimal core contains exactly these five components. ∎

The full six-graph system is an optional enrichment layer that becomes mandatory only when the claim is to be aggregated with other claims, compared across conflicts, or used for causal inference.

