Chapter 4: The Evidence Chain and Provenance Architecture

4.1 The Evidence Chain

Every important numerical claim should be traceable through:

\boxed{ \text{Claim} \rightarrow \text{Source} \rightarrow \text{Passage} \rightarrow \text{Definition} \rightarrow \text{Scope} \rightarrow \text{Method} \rightarrow \text{Verification} }

A secondary source repeating a primary source does not create an independent observation. Therefore:

n\text{ secondary repetitions} \neq n\text{ independent observations}

If ten reports reproduce the same underlying institutional estimate:

\boxed{ 10\text{ reports}\rightarrow1\text{ underlying evidence source} }

unless independent data or methodology are demonstrated.

4.2 The PROV-O Provenance Model

The W3C PROV Ontology (PROV-O) provides a formal standard for representing provenance information using the OWL2 Web Ontology Language. PROV-O is a lightweight OWL-RL ontology that encodes PROV-DM's abstract model in RDF and is domain-neutral and meant to be extended. The core elements are:

Entities are physical, digital, conceptual, or other kinds of thing.

Activities are something that actually occurred over a period of time and acts upon or with entities.

Agents are something or someone which was responsible for or otherwise associated with what happened in an activity.

Key properties include wasGeneratedBy, wasDerivedFrom, wasAttributedTo, used, and wasInformedBy. One advantage of PROV-O is that it is based on semantic web standards, so provenance information can be interpreted by machines without ambiguity using well-defined semantics.

4.3 ISO 19115 Data Quality Elements

ISO 19115 specifies data quality elements including:

Lineage describes the history of a dataset and recounts the life cycle from collection and acquisition through compilation and derivation to its current form. Every metadata entry shall provide basic lineage information, with process steps including description, rationale, and dateTime stamp.

Completeness is the presence and absence of features, their attributes and relationships, with sub-elements for commission (excess data) and omission (missing data).

Logical consistency is the degree of adherence to logical rules of data structure, attribution and relationships.

Thematic accuracy is the accuracy of quantitative attributes and correctness of non-quantitative attributes.

Temporal quality is the accuracy of temporal attributes and temporal relationships.

4.4 Verification Statuses

A number without sufficient provenance receives an appropriate verification status:

· UNVERIFIED: no provenance chain established.
· VERIFIED_PRIMARY: derived directly from a primary source with complete provenance.
· VERIFIED_SECONDARY: derived from a secondary source that reproduces a primary source.
· DERIVED_VERIFIED: derived from verified inputs through a traceable transformation.
· REJECTED: provenance chain is broken or evidence contradicts the claim.

Proposition 4.1 (Backward Propagation of Verification). If $x_j = f(x_1, \ldots, x_n)$ and $x_j$ is DERIVED_VERIFIED, then each $x_i$ must have a verification status of at least VERIFIED_SECONDARY.

Proof. By Axiom 3.1 (Dependency Closure), each input $x_i$ must be traceable. By the definition of DERIVED_VERIFIED, all inputs must be traceable and verifiable. Therefore, each input must have at least secondary verification. ∎

4.5 Formal Provenance Partial Order

Definition 4.1 (Provenance Partial Order). Define a partial order $\preceq_P$ on Measurement Objects: $M_i \preceq_P M_j$ if $M_j$ depends on $M_i$ in the provenance chain.

Proposition 4.2 (Monotonicity of Verification). Verification status is monotone with respect to $\preceq_P$: if $M_i \preceq_P M_j$, then $\operatorname{Status}(M_i) \succeq \operatorname{Status}(M_j)$.

Proof. If $M_j$ depends on $M_i$, then the reliability of $M_j$ cannot exceed the reliability of $M_i$. Backward propagation follows from the definition of dependency. ∎

