Chapter 5: The Six-Graph Architecture

5.1 Overview

\boxed{ \mathcal G= (G_C,G_B,G_O,G_D,G_M,G_{CF}) }

Each graph answers a different question and operates at a different logical level.

5.2 The Causal Graph $G_C$

5.2.1 Formal Definition

G_C=(V,E_C,\alpha)

where:

· $V$ is a set of variables (nodes);
· $E_C \subseteq V \times V \times \mathcal{T}_C$ is a set of typed causal edges;
· $\alpha: E_C \rightarrow \mathbb{R} \cup \{\bot\}$ assigns an effect magnitude to each edge, where $\bot$ denotes an unidentified effect.

5.2.2 Typed Causal Edges

Each edge $i \rightarrow j$ in $G_C$ has a type $t \in \mathcal{T}_C$ where:

\mathcal{T}_C = \{\text{IDENTIFIED}, \text{SUPPOSED}, \text{THEORETICAL}\}

Type A — Causalité identifiée (IDENTIFIED). The edge is supported by an explicit identification strategy. The effect $\beta_{ij}$ is estimated with quantified uncertainty.

Type B — Causalité supposée (SUPPOSED). The edge is postulated on the basis of theoretical reasoning or documented mechanisms, but is not empirically identified in the case considered.

Type C — Causalité théorique (THEORETICAL). The edge represents a hypothetical relationship from a theoretical model, without empirical validation.

5.2.3 Identification Assumptions

For a Type A edge to be valid, the identification assumptions must be declared. In the DAG framework, two principal assumptions are required:

Local Markov assumption: a node is independent of all its non-descendants conditional on its parents.

Causal edge assumption: arrows represent exclusively direct causal relationships.

Pearl's do-calculus provides three rules for transforming interventional distributions into observational ones. The ID algorithm provides a sound, complete and computationally efficient solution for the problem of identifying interventional distributions from observational data, given assumptions encoded in a causal diagram. Do-calculus was proved sound and complete for general queries of the form $P(Y|\mathrm{do}(X))$.

5.2.4 Formal Properties

Proposition 5.1 (Edge Type Hierarchy). The set of edge types forms a total order under epistemic strength:

\text{IDENTIFIED} \succ \text{SUPPOSED} \succ \text{THEORETICAL}

Only edges of type IDENTIFIED may be used for quantitative causal inference without sensitivity qualification.

Proof. By the definitions of the edge types: IDENTIFIED edges have passed empirical identification tests, SUPPOSED edges rest on theoretical plausibility without empirical identification, and THEORETICAL edges are purely hypothetical. The epistemic warrant decreases monotonically along this ordering. ∎

Proposition 5.2 (Causal vs. Amplification Distinction). For every edge $i \rightarrow j$:

\text{Causal relationship} \neq \text{Amplification relationship}

A causal effect may attenuate, reverse sign, or depend on state. The framework does not require $\alpha_{ij} \geq 1$ for every causal relationship.

Proof. By counterexample: increased military expenditure (cause) may lead to decreased civilian consumption (effect with $\alpha < 1$). The causal relationship is valid, but the amplification ratio is less than one. ∎

5.3 The Accounting Graph $G_B$

5.3.1 Formal Definition

G_B=(V,E_B)

where $E_B$ represents relationships created by accounting identities, budgets and financial statements.

5.3.2 The Fundamental Distinction

\boxed{ G_B \neq G_C }

A variable can enter an accounting identity without causing another variable. The national income accounting identity $Y = C + I + G + (X - M)$ establishes relationships between output, consumption, investment, government spending, and net exports. These are definitional identities, not causal claims.

5.3.3 Accounting Identity Types

Definition 5.2 (Accounting Identity). An accounting identity is a relation $A(\mathbf{x}) = 0$ that holds by definition for any admissible values of the vector $\mathbf{x}$.

Axiom 5.1 (Identity Independence). No accounting identity in $G_B$ may be interpreted as a causal claim without independent causal identification.

Proposition 5.3. The accounting graph $G_B$ and causal graph $G_C$ may share nodes but not edges. If an edge exists in both, it must be independently justified in each graph.

Proof. An edge in $G_B$ represents an accounting relation. An edge in $G_C$ represents a causal relation. These are logically distinct types of relation. Co-occurrence requires independent justification. ∎

5.4 The Overlap Graph $G_O$

5.4.1 Formal Definition

G_O=(V,E_O,O)

where $O_{ij}\geq0$ represents an identified or bounded overlap between two measured cost components.

5.4.2 The Fundamental Rule

\boxed{ \text{Causal dependence} \neq \text{Accounting overlap} }

Two costs can be causally related without being double-counted. Conversely, two independently reported costs can contain the same monetary component.

5.4.3 Typed Overlap Measures

Definition 5.3 (Overlap Types). An overlap measure $O_{ij}$ has one of the following types:

Set-theoretic overlap: $O_{ij}$ is a well-defined set intersection, suitable for inclusion-exclusion.

Monetary overlap: $O_{ij}$ is a monetary amount representing shared expenditure or valuation.

Bounded overlap: $O_{ij}$ is an interval $[\underline{O}_{ij},\overline{O}_{ij}]$ representing uncertainty about the degree of overlap.

Qualitative flag: $O_{ij}$ indicates that overlap may exist but cannot be quantified.

5.4.4 The Inclusion-Exclusion Formula

When a genuine set-like overlap measure exists:

C_{\cup} = \sum_i C_i - \sum_{i<j}O_{ij} + \sum_{i<j<k}O_{ijk} - \cdots

Proposition 5.4 (Admissibility of Inclusion-Exclusion). The inclusion-exclusion formula is valid only when the underlying quantities possess a well-defined set-like overlap measure.

Proof. The inclusion-exclusion principle derives from set theory. For it to apply, the quantities must be elements of a Boolean algebra with well-defined intersections. Economic indicators do not generally satisfy this condition. ∎

5.5 The Data-Dependency Graph $G_D$

5.5.1 Formal Definition

G_D=(X,E_D)

where $X$ is a set of quantities and $E_D$ is a set of edges $X_i \rightarrow X_j$ meaning that $X_j$ depends mathematically or statistically on $X_i$.

5.5.2 Formal Properties

Axiom 5.2 (Acyclicity). $G_D$ is a directed acyclic graph.

Proposition 5.5. For $X_3=f(X_1,X_2)$, the framework records $X_1\rightarrow X_3$ and $X_2\rightarrow X_3$.

Proposition 5.6 (Verification Propagation). Verification propagates backward through the dependency graph.

Proof. If $X_j$ depends on $X_i$, then the reliability of $X_j$ cannot exceed the reliability of $X_i$. Backward propagation follows from the definition of dependency. ∎

5.6 The Measurement-Compatibility Graph $G_M$

5.6.1 Formal Definition

G_M=(V,E_M,\mathcal C)

where $\mathcal C$ assigns to each pair a compatibility vector:

\mathcal C_{ij} = (c_{\rm object}, c_{\rm valuation}, c_{\rm time}, c_{\rm unit}, c_{\rm scope}, c_{\rm cf})

where:

· $c_{\rm object}$: accounting object;
· $c_{\rm valuation}$: valuation basis;
· $c_{\rm time}$: temporal horizon;
· $c_{\rm unit}$: unit/currency;
· $c_{\rm scope}$: population/system boundary;
· $c_{\rm cf}$: counterfactual basis where relevant.

5.6.2 The Fundamental Distinction

\boxed{ \text{Non-comparability} \neq \text{Double counting} }

\boxed{ G_M \neq G_O }

Two quantities may be non-comparable without being double-counted, and vice versa.

5.6.3 Graded Compatibility

Rather than a binary $\operatorname{Comparable}(i,j)$, compatibility is assessed on a graded scale:

\operatorname{Comp}(i,j) \in \{ \text{ExactMatch}, \text{CompatibleAfterAdjustment}, \text{QualitativelyComparable}, \text{NonComparable} \}

Proposition 5.7 (Partial Order of Compatibility). The compatibility levels form a partial order:

\text{ExactMatch} \succeq \text{CompatibleAfterAdjustment} \succeq \text{QualitativelyComparable} \succeq \text{NonComparable}

Proof. ExactMatch implies no adjustment needed. CompatibleAfterAdjustment implies adjustments are possible but required. QualitativelyComparable implies only ordinal comparison is valid. NonComparable implies no valid comparison. The implication chain establishes the partial order. ∎

5.7 The Counterfactual-Design Graph $G_{CF}$

5.7.1 Formal Definition

\boxed{ D_{CF}= (U_T,U_D,Y,T,I,\mathcal I) }

where:

· $U_T$: treated unit;
· $U_D$: donor/comparison set;
· $Y$: outcome;
· $T$: temporal domain;
· $I$: intervention/conflict;
· $\mathcal I$: identification assumptions.

5.7.2 The Synthetic Control Method

The Synthetic Control Method (SCM) constructs a counterfactual outcome via a convex combination of control units with optimal weights constrained to be nonnegative and summed to one. For each posttreatment period, the estimated treatment effect is the difference between observed and counterfactual outcomes.

Application to Ukraine. A study applying SCM to estimate Ukraine's economic output loss from 2014–2023 created a "synthetic Ukraine" from a weighted mix of 18 regional peer countries, revealing a large, growing GDP gap after 2014. The cumulative loss was estimated at approximately $1.1 trillion in nominal GDP (PPP) over the period 2014–2023.

5.7.3 Placebo Testing

The credibility of SCM estimates depends on placebo tests.

In-space placebo test: uses each control unit as a fake treatment unit in turn to estimate a distribution of placebo effects.

In-time placebo test: applies SCM using a fake treatment time prior to the actual treatment time.

Mixed placebo test: combines both in-time and in-space placebo tests.

5.7.4 Limitations of SCM

The SCM literature identifies significant vulnerabilities. An intervention may have spillover effects on units that are not directly targeted by it. If units affected by spillover effects are included in the synthetic control, the researcher should be aware of the potential direction of the bias of the resulting estimator. A primary challenge to unbiased estimation using synthetic control is treatment spillover, which can contaminate both never-treated units in the donor pool and to-be-treated units in a staggered implementation.

5.7.5 Pre-registration of Counterfactual Designs

The specification of the counterfactual design must be declared before analysis. Pre-registration of hypotheses and planned methods, including covariate adjustments, modeling choices, and subgroup analyses, helps distinguish between confirmatory and exploratory inferences.

