Chapter 7: The Aggregation Architecture

7.1 The Aggregation Problem

Let $S=\{C_1,\ldots,C_n\}$ be a set of cost components. An aggregate is admissible only if the components satisfy the compatibility requirements relevant to the target estimand.

7.2 Boolean Admissibility

\boxed{ \operatorname{Agg}(S)=1 }

only when:

\begin{cases} \text{scope is explicitly defined} \\ \text{valuation bases are compatible} \\ \text{time horizons are compatible} \\ \text{units are compatible} \\ \text{overlaps are identified or bounded} \\ \text{counterfactual requirements are satisfied when applicable} \\ \text{dependencies are traceable} \\ \text{uncertainty is propagated} \end{cases}

7.3 Graded Admissibility

Real cases often have partial admissibility. A graded admissibility score is preferable:

\operatorname{Agg}(S) = (a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_8) \in [0,1]^8

where each component represents the degree to which a specific admissibility condition is satisfied.

Definition 7.1 (Admissibility Function). The admissibility function is defined as:

\operatorname{Agg}(S) = \bigwedge_{i=1}^{8} a_i

where $\wedge$ denotes the minimum operator. The aggregate is fully admissible if $\operatorname{Agg}(S) = 1$. The aggregate is partially admissible if $0 < \operatorname{Agg}(S) < 1$ with explicit failure flags indicating which conditions are not met. The aggregate is inadmissible if $\operatorname{Agg}(S) = 0$.

7.4 The Two Aggregation Architectures

Additive architecture:

C_{\rm additive} = \sum_i C_i - \text{identified overlaps}

Strength: mechanism transparency. Risk: double counting.

Unified architecture:

C_{\rm unified} = Y_{\rm counterfactual} - Y_{\rm observed}

for an appropriately defined aggregate outcome $Y$. Strength: system-level integration. Risk: counterfactual misspecification.

Proposition 7.1 (Non-Universality of Aggregation Architecture). Neither additive nor unified architecture is universally superior. The choice is methodological.

\boxed{ \text{Methodological choice} \neq \text{empirical hypothesis} }

Proof. By counterexample: for a narrowly defined estimand such as "replacement cost of destroyed bridges," additive aggregation is appropriate and unified counterfactual estimation is unnecessary. For a broadly defined estimand such as "total economic welfare loss," unified counterfactual estimation is necessary and additive aggregation risks double counting. ∎

7.5 The Measurement Theory Foundation

Stevens introduced a classification of scales based on their permissible transformations. The class of permissible transformations is the set of all affine transformations for interval scales, all linear transformations for ratio scales, and isotonic or order-preserving transformations for ordinal scales. A scale of measurement is a mapping which assigns real numbers to objects being measured, and the type of a scale is defined by a class of admissible transformations.

Theorem 7.1 (Aggregation Invariance). For any aggregation function $\operatorname{Agg}$, meaningfulness requires that $\operatorname{Agg}$ be invariant under the admissible transformation group of the scale on which its inputs are measured.

Proof. This is a standard result in measurement theory. An aggregation function that changes its qualitative conclusions under an admissible transformation is not measuring a real property of the system. For interval scales, the admissible transformations are affine, so only aggregation functions invariant under affine transformations are meaningful. For ratio scales, the admissible transformations are similarity transformations, so only aggregation functions invariant under similarity transformations are meaningful. ∎

Corollary 7.1. Additive aggregation is admissible only for ratio or absolute scales.

