Chapter 10: Uncertainty Propagation

10.1 The Uncertainty Problem

Uncertainty propagation is asserted but not given a concrete treatment. This chapter provides a Bayesian hierarchical or interval-propagation treatment that specifies how uncertainty flows through the dependency structure.

10.2 Types of Uncertainty

The framework distinguishes:

· Measurement uncertainty: arising from imprecise observation or recording.
· Parameter uncertainty: arising from estimation of model parameters.
· Model uncertainty: arising from the choice of model structure.
· Counterfactual uncertainty: arising from the construction of the counterfactual.
· Scenario uncertainty: arising from the choice of scenario assumptions.

10.3 Interval Propagation

For a transformation $Y = f(X_1, \ldots, X_n)$ where each $X_i \in [\underline{X}_i, \overline{X}_i]$:

\underline{Y} = \min_{X_i \in [\underline{X}_i, \overline{X}_i]} f(X_1, \ldots, X_n)

\overline{Y} = \max_{X_i \in [\underline{X}_i, \overline{X}_i]} f(X_1, \ldots, X_n)

Proposition 10.1 (Monotonicity of Interval Propagation). If $f$ is monotone increasing in each argument, then:

\underline{Y} = f(\underline{X}_1, \ldots, \underline{X}_n)

\overline{Y} = f(\overline{X}_1, \ldots, \overline{X}_n)

Proof. For a monotone increasing function, the minimum is attained at the lower bounds and the maximum at the upper bounds. ∎

10.4 Bayesian Hierarchical Propagation

When probabilistic information is available, a Bayesian hierarchical model can be specified:

Y \sim p(Y | \theta)

\theta \sim p(\theta | \phi)

\phi \sim p(\phi)

The posterior predictive distribution $p(Y | \text{data})$ provides a full characterization of uncertainty.

10.5 Propagation Through the Dependency Graph

Definition 10.1 (Uncertainty Propagation Function). For each edge $X_i \rightarrow X_j$ in $G_D$, define a propagation function:

U_j = g_{ij}(U_i, U_{\rm local})

where $U_i$ is the uncertainty of $X_i$ and $U_{\rm local}$ is the local uncertainty introduced at $X_j$.

Proposition 10.2 (Uncertainty Monotonicity). Uncertainty is monotone with respect to the dependency graph: if $X_i \rightarrow X_j$, then $U_j \geq U_i$ unless $X_j$ is observed directly.

Proof. If $X_j$ depends on $X_i$, then the uncertainty of $X_j$ cannot be less than the uncertainty of $X_i$ unless additional information about $X_j$ is available. ∎

10.6 The Uncertainty Budget

Every aggregate should be accompanied by an uncertainty budget that decomposes total uncertainty into its components:

U_{\rm total} = U_{\rm measurement} + U_{\rm parameter} + U_{\rm model} + U_{\rm counterfactual} + U_{\rm scenario}

This budget makes transparent which sources of uncertainty dominate and where additional data collection or model refinement would be most valuable.

