Chapter 11: The Falsification Architecture

11.1 The Falsification Principle

The design pattern is founded on one methodological principle:

\boxed{ \textbf{ Do not ask the data to confirm the architecture. Expose the architecture to cases capable of breaking it. } }

A theory that survives only the case from which it was constructed has not demonstrated generality.

11.2 The Generalization Test

Define a set of conflicts $\mathcal K = \{k_1, k_2, \ldots, k_n\}$. Generalization is supported only if the architecture demonstrates sufficient portability across heterogeneous cases.

Test A — Structural Portability. Can the architecture represent the relevant causal, accounting, overlap, data, measurement and counterfactual structures?

G^{(k)}_C, G^{(k)}_B, G^{(k)}_O, G^{(k)}_D, G_M^{(k)}, G_{CF}^{(k)}

for the relevant estimands? Failure may indicate a missing architectural component.

Test B — Module Completeness. For each conflict, identify important mechanisms that cannot be represented by existing modules. If a recurring mechanism repeatedly escapes the architecture:

\boxed{ \text{architecture revision required} }

rather than forcing the mechanism into an inappropriate module.

Test C — Parameter Generality. For a parameter $p$, test whether $p_k$ is universal, class-specific, conflict-specific, or structurally unstable.

11.3 The Failure Taxonomy

When the framework fails, the failure must be classified:

· Type I — Data failure: Required evidence does not exist or cannot be verified.
· Type II — Definition failure: The target quantity is ambiguously defined.
· Type III — Measurement incompatibility: Quantities cannot legitimately be compared or aggregated.
· Type IV — Accounting overlap: The same monetary object appears in multiple components.
· Type V — Causal identification failure: The proposed causal relationship cannot be identified.
· Type VI — Counterfactual failure: The alternative trajectory cannot be credibly constructed.
· Type VII — Model failure: The assumed mathematical structure does not reproduce relevant observations.
· Type VIII — Module failure: An important conflict mechanism is absent from the architecture.
· Type IX — Generalization failure: The architecture works in one conflict class but breaks systematically in another.

\boxed{ \text{Failure} \neq \text{Defeat} }

A failure can reveal exactly what the architecture needs to improve.

11.4 The Failure Decision Tree

Definition 11.1 (Failure Decision Tree). The failure taxonomy is represented as a decision tree with explicit branching conditions:

If data exists but cannot be verified $\rightarrow$ Type I.

If data is verified but the estimand is ambiguous $\rightarrow$ Type II.

If the estimand is clear but measurements are incompatible $\rightarrow$ Type III.

If measurements are compatible but overlaps are unresolved $\rightarrow$ Type IV.

If overlaps are resolved but causal identification fails $\rightarrow$ Type V.

If causal identification succeeds but counterfactual construction fails $\rightarrow$ Type VI.

If counterfactual construction succeeds but model fit fails $\rightarrow$ Type VII.

If model fit succeeds but a mechanism is missing $\rightarrow$ Type VIII.

If all mechanisms are covered but generalization fails $\rightarrow$ Type IX.

Proposition 11.1 (Exhaustiveness). The failure decision tree is exhaustive for the class of admissible Measurement Objects.

Proof. The tree partitions the space of failure modes according to the first condition that fails in the measurement pipeline. Since the pipeline is sequential and the conditions are mutually exclusive at each stage, every failure mode is classified by exactly one type. ∎

