Chapter 12: The Structured Comparison Methodology

12.1 The Comparison Problem

The dissertation contains no executed application that follows the full pre-registration protocol. The Iraq and Ukraine passages are illustrative, not architecture-level audits. Until at least one complete, pre-registered application exists—including documented failures—the claim of empirical falsifiability is aspirational.

This chapter provides a structured comparison methodology that can be applied to published estimates without requiring new data collection.

12.2 The Comparison Protocol

Protocol 12.1 (Structured Comparison of Published Estimates). To compare $n$ published estimates of the same conflict:

Step 1. For each estimate, construct a minimal core Measurement Object:

M_i^{\rm core} = (V_i, T_i, P_i, S_i, \operatorname{Status}_i)

Step 2. Identify the estimand $\theta_i^*$ for each estimate.

Step 3. Construct the compatibility matrix $\mathcal C_{ij}$ for all pairs.

Step 4. Identify overlaps $O_{ij}$ for all pairs.

Step 5. Classify each failure mode according to the failure taxonomy.

Step 6. Report the sources of non-comparability transparently.

12.3 Application to the Iraq Case

The Iraq case illustrates the methodology. The CBO estimate and the Stiglitz–Bilmes estimate differ in:

· Scope: CBO restricted to federal budgetary costs; Stiglitz–Bilmes included veterans' costs, family costs, and macroeconomic losses.
· Valuation: CBO used then-year dollars; Stiglitz–Bilmes used present-value dollars discounted at 4.5 percent.
· Time horizon: CBO covered 2003–2007; Stiglitz–Bilmes projected to 2017.

When placed on a comparable basis, the apparent disparity narrows substantially. The CBO reports $604 billion through October 2007; Stiglitz–Bilmes report $646 billion when inflated to 2007 dollars.

12.4 Application to the Ukraine Case

The Ukraine case illustrates the counterfactual methodology. The SCM study constructs a "synthetic Ukraine" from 18 donor countries. The cumulative loss is estimated at approximately $1.1 trillion in nominal GDP (PPP) for 2014–2023.

The structured comparison would identify:

· Counterfactual design: SCM with donor weights concentrated on Armenia (0.54), Romania (0.35), and Hungary (0.10).
· Placebo tests: Armenia as primary placebo case.
· Limitations: Spillover effects from the conflict may contaminate donor units.

12.5 The Value of Structured Comparison

The structured comparison methodology demonstrates the architecture's value without requiring new data collection. It makes the sources of non-comparability fully transparent and allows independent researchers to audit one another's estimates.

