Chapter 13: The Modular Architecture and MCO Embedding

13.1 The Modular Architecture

\boxed{ \text{International Architecture} + \text{Conflict-Specific Modules} + \text{Conflict-Specific Parameters} }

The UN, a development bank, a government, or a university need not use exactly the same economic models. They could use the same measurement grammar.

13.2 The Module Library

A preliminary module library is:

\mathcal M= \{ M_E,M_M,M_T,M_I,M_H,M_F,M_{FI}, M_S,M_R,M_{Env},M_C,M_{Rec},M_L,M_{Ag},M_{Tr},M_{Inf} \}

representing: $M_E$: energy; $M_M$: maritime; $M_T$: trade; $M_I$: infrastructure; $M_H$: human capital; $M_F$: fiscal; $M_{FI}$: financial; $M_S$: health; $M_R$: migration; $M_{Env}$: environment; $M_C$: cyber; $M_{Rec}$: reconstruction; $M_L$: long-term scarring; $M_{Ag}$: agriculture and food security; $M_{Tr}$: local trade and market networks; $M_{Inf}$: infrastructure rehabilitation.

For conflict $k$:

\boxed{ \mathcal M_k\subseteq\mathcal M }

13.3 The MCO Embedding

Hartley's Modern Conflict Ontology (MCO) creates a structure for collecting and analyzing information regarding both conventional and unconventional conflict in the face of uncertainty. Actors are defined as the main elements of the conceptualization, as they can be direct or indirect stakeholders in modern conflicts, they can take actions and be subject to the actions of other actors, while influencing or being influenced by the objects of modern conflicts. Actions are defined as interventions, events or processes that can be classified as diplomatic, information, military, or economic actions.

Theorem 13.1 (MCO Embedding). The MCO can be embedded into the measurement architecture by mapping MCO entities to nodes in $G_C$ and $G_B$, and MCO relations to typed edges.

Proof. MCO actors map to nodes in $G_C$ (causal agents) and $G_B$ (accounting entities). MCO actions map to typed edges: diplomatic actions to $G_C$ edges with type SUPPOSED or IDENTIFIED, economic actions to $G_B$ edges. MCO objects map to nodes in $G_D$ (data dependencies). The embedding preserves the structural constraints of MCO as a special case of the measurement architecture. ∎

Correspondence Table.

MCO Element Measurement Architecture Element Preservation
Actor Node in $G_C$ and $G_B$ Structural
Diplomatic action Edge in $G_C$ (SUPPOSED/IDENTIFIED) Typed
Economic action Edge in $G_B$ Typed
Military action Edge in $G_C$ (IDENTIFIED) Typed
Information action Edge in $G_C$ (SUPPOSED) Typed
Object Node in $G_D$ Structural

What is preserved. The MCO's structural constraints on actors, actions, and objects are preserved as a special case of the measurement architecture.

What is added. The measurement architecture adds typed provenance, verification status, compatibility grading, overlap classification, and counterfactual design—none of which are part of the MCO.

Division of labor. The MCO is a structuring ontology for modeling and simulation. The measurement architecture is a measurement and audit system. The MCO provides the conceptual vocabulary; the measurement architecture provides the measurement discipline.

