Chapter 15: The Minimal Core and Open Research Programs

15.1 The Minimal Core Measurement Object

The full architecture requires six graphs, provenance chains, compatibility vectors, and counterfactual designs. This is theoretically complete but operationally heavy. The minimal core Measurement Object defined in Chapter 3 is sufficient for a single, non-aggregated numerical claim:

M_i^{\rm core} = (V_i, T_i, P_i, S_i, \operatorname{Status}_i)

The full six-graph system is an optional enrichment layer that becomes mandatory only when the claim is to be aggregated with other claims, compared across conflicts, or used for causal inference.

15.2 The Integration Path with Existing Standards

The architecture can be integrated with existing statistical standards:

· PROV-O for provenance representation;
· ISO 19115 for data quality assessment;
· SNA 2008 for national accounts integration;
· SEEA for environmental-economic accounting.

This integration path gives the architecture institutional legitimacy and interoperability without requiring institutions to abandon their existing standards.

15.3 The Functorial Measurement Theory as an Open Research Program

The move from graphs-as-structures to a functor $\mathcal{F}: \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{A}$ is ambitious and potentially powerful. However, as currently formulated, it risks reading as ornamental rather than operational. The definitions of $\mathcal{M}$ and $\mathcal{A}$ are thin; the three preservation properties are stated but not derived from the earlier axioms; and the natural-transformation interpretation of empirical applications is more metaphor than proof.

We therefore demote the functorial measurement theory to an explicit open research program.

Open Problem 15.1 (Category of Measurement Objects). Define a category $\mathcal{M}$ whose objects are Measurement Objects and whose morphisms are admissible transformations. Specify composition, identity, and associativity.

Open Problem 15.2 (Category of Admissible Aggregates). Define a category $\mathcal{A}$ whose objects are admissible aggregates and whose morphisms are refinement relations.

Open Conjecture 15.3 (Functorial Measurement Theory). There exists a functor $\mathcal{F}: \mathcal{M} \rightarrow \mathcal{A}$ such that:

1. $\mathcal{F}$ preserves typing;
2. $\mathcal{F}$ preserves provenance;
3. $\mathcal{F}$ is faithful.

This conjecture is stated as a research program, not as an established result. Its resolution would transform the framework from a descriptive taxonomy into a mathematical theory of measurement. Its failure would not invalidate the architecture; it would simply mean that the architecture is not functorial.

15.4 The Reference Implementation as a Research Program

A lightweight open-source reference implementation (R or Python) that accepts Measurement Objects, constructs the relevant graphs, checks graded admissibility, and produces an audit report would greatly enhance the architecture's usability. Visualization of the six graphs is essential for usability and critique.

This is an engineering task, not a theoretical contribution. It is listed here as a research program rather than as a completed component of the dissertation.

15.5 The Empirical Stress-Test as a Research Program

The four-conflict set and the "unseen conflict" test are correctly emphasized, yet the dissertation itself contains no executed application that follows the full pre-registration protocol. Until at least one complete, pre-registered application exists—including documented failures—the claim of empirical falsifiability is aspirational.

Research Program 15.6 (Empirical Stress-Test). Pre-register the architecture itself (estimands, modules, falsification tests) and apply it to one genuinely unseen or historical case with contested data (e.g., a phase of the Yugoslav wars, Vietnam, or a non-state conflict). Document every Type I–IX failure.

