Chapter 16: Conclusion

16.1 The Central Proposition

\boxed{ \textbf{ A conflict-cost estimate should be treated not as a number, but as a structured measurement object. } }

Formally:

\boxed{ \mathfrak E_k= ( \theta_k, \mathcal E_k, \mathcal X_k, \mathcal G_k, \mathcal C_k, \mathcal A_k, \mathcal V_k, \mathcal U_k ) }

where $\mathfrak E_k$ contains not only the result, but the evidentiary, causal, accounting, counterfactual, aggregation, valuation and uncertainty structures required to interpret it.

\boxed{ \text{Estimate} = \text{Value} + \text{Provenance} + \text{Definitions} + \text{Assumptions} + \text{Dependencies} + \text{Uncertainty} }

16.2 The Design Pattern 01

\boxed{ \text{A conflict-cost claim cannot enter an aggregate until its measurement type, provenance, scope, compatibility and dependency structure are explicitly declared.} }

16.3 Final Formulation

\boxed{ \textbf{One measurement architecture. Many conflicts.} }

\boxed{ \textbf{Mechanisms and parameters remain empirical.} }

\boxed{ \textbf{Every estimate remains auditable and falsifiable.} }

16.4 Fundamental Invariants

\boxed{ \text{Observation} \neq \text{Estimate} \neq \text{Derived} \neq \text{Counterfactual} }

\boxed{ \text{Causality} \neq \text{Accounting} }

\boxed{ \text{Overlap} \neq \text{Non-comparability} }

\boxed{ \text{Network share} \neq \text{Amplification} }

\boxed{ \text{Monetary cost} \neq \text{Welfare loss} }

\boxed{ \text{Methodological rule} \neq \text{Empirical hypothesis} }

\boxed{ \text{Precision} \neq \text{Accuracy} }

16.5 The Research Program

\boxed{ \text{Case Study} \rightarrow \text{Mechanism Extraction} \rightarrow \text{Formal Abstraction} \rightarrow \text{Independent Replication} \rightarrow \text{Cross-Conflict Testing} \rightarrow \text{Architecture Revision} }

16.6 Closing Statement

The ambition of this work is not to produce a universal number for the cost of human conflict. It is to establish a disciplined scientific language through which different societies, institutions and researchers can construct, inspect, challenge and compare such measurements.

\boxed{ \textbf{ One architecture. Many conflicts. Different mechanisms. Transparent assumptions. Falsifiable results. } }


