Appendix I: Mathematical Foundations Reference

I.1 Measurement Theory

Definition I.1 (Scale Type). A scale type is a triple $(\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{G}, \phi)$ where $\mathcal{S}$ is a set of measurements, $\mathcal{G}$ is a group of admissible transformations, and $\phi: \mathcal{S} \rightarrow \mathcal{G}$ is a representation function.

Definition I.2 (Meaningfulness). A statement involving measurements is meaningful if its truth value is invariant under all admissible transformations.

Theorem I.1 (Stevens' Classification). The scale types are:

· Nominal: admissible transformations are permutations.
· Ordinal: admissible transformations are isotonic (monotone increasing).
· Interval: admissible transformations are affine ($x \mapsto ax + b$, $a > 0$).
· Ratio: admissible transformations are similarity ($x \mapsto ax$, $a > 0$).
· Absolute: admissible transformations are trivial (identity only).

I.2 Causal Inference

Definition I.3 (Causal Graph). A causal graph is a directed acyclic graph $G = (V, E)$ where nodes represent variables and edges represent direct causal relationships.

Definition I.4 (d-Separation). Two nodes $X$ and $Y$ are d-separated by a set $Z$ if every path between $X$ and $Y$ is blocked by $Z$.

Theorem I.2 (Markov Property). In a causal graph $G$, if $X$ and $Y$ are d-separated by $Z$, then $X \perp Y \mid Z$ in the observational distribution.

Theorem I.3 (Do-Calculus). The three rules of do-calculus are sound and complete for identifying interventional distributions from observational data given a causal graph.

I.3 Synthetic Control Method

Definition I.5 (Synthetic Control). Given a treated unit $T$ and donor pool $D = \{1, \ldots, J\}$, the synthetic control is:
\hat{Y}_T = \sum_{j=1}^J w_j Y_j


where $w_j \geq 0$ and $\sum_j w_j = 1$.

Definition I.6 (Optimal Weights). The optimal weights $w^*$ minimize:
\|X_T - \sum_j w_j X_j\|


subject to $w_j \geq 0$ and $\sum_j w_j = 1$, where $X_T$ and $X_j$ are pre-treatment characteristics.

Theorem I.4 (Bias of SCM). The SCM estimator is unbiased if and only if the convex hull condition and the no-spillover condition hold.

I.4 Welfare Economics

Definition I.7 (Social Welfare Function). A social welfare function is a function $\mathcal{W}: \mathbb{R}^n \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ mapping individual utilities to a social welfare level.

Definition I.8 (Utilitarian SWF). $\mathcal{W}(U_1, \ldots, U_n) = \sum_i U_i$.

Definition I.9 (Prioritarian SWF). $\mathcal{W}(U_1, \ldots, U_n) = \sum_i \phi(U_i)$ where $\phi$ is strictly increasing and strictly concave.

Theorem I.5 (Pigou-Dalton Principle). A SWF satisfies the Pigou-Dalton principle if any transfer from a better-off to a worse-off individual that does not reverse their ranking increases social welfare.

Proposition I.1. Utilitarian SWFs satisfy the Pigou-Dalton principle only if the utility function is concave. Prioritarian SWFs satisfy it by construction.

